{"name":"Chanticleer","hex":"#870000","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chanticleer","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A domestic rooster or cock, especially in fables and fairy tales."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make the crowing sound of a cock.","To crow in exultation."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chanticleer"}],"entries":[{"title":"Reynard the Fox","blocks":[{"text":"Reynard the Fox is a literary cycle of medieval allegorical Dutch, English, French and German fables. The first extant versions of the cycle date from the second half of the 12th century. The genre was popular throughout the Late Middle Ages, as well as in chapbook form throughout the Early Modern period."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Roman.de.renart.2.jpg/330px-Roman.de.renart.2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reynard_the_Fox","wikidata":"Q505194"},{"title":"The Nun's Priest's Tale","blocks":[{"text":"\"The Nun's Priest's Tale of the Cock and Hen, Chauntecleer and Pertelote\" is a narrative poem and one of The Canterbury Tales by Middle English poet Geoffrey Chaucer. Composed in the 1390s, it is a beast fable and mock epic based on an incident in the Reynard cycle. The story of Chanticleer and the Fox became further popularised in Britain through this means."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/The_Nun%27s_Priest_-_Ellesmere_Chaucer.jpg/330px-The_Nun%27s_Priest_-_Ellesmere_Chaucer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Nun's_Priest's_Tale","wikidata":"Q3795398"},{"title":"Chanticleer (ensemble)","blocks":[{"text":"Chanticleer is an American male classical vocal ensemble based in San Francisco, California, founded in 1978. It is known for its interpretations of Renaissance music, for which they were founded, but also a wide repertoire of jazz, gospel and contemporary classical music. Its name is derived from the \"clear singing rooster\" in Chaucer's The Canterbury Tales. The ensemble has made award-winning recordings."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Chanticleer2014-Formal-1.jpg/330px-Chanticleer2014-Formal-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chanticleer_(ensemble)","wikidata":"Q1062439"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Fox_preaching_to_rooster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Fox_preaching_to_rooster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"A defaced Reynard preaches to a rooster.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFox_preaching_to_rooster.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/Fuchs.margin_%28MMW10F50_f6r%29_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/Fuchs.margin_%28MMW10F50_f6r%29_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"A studious fox in a monk's cowl, in the margins of a book of hours, Utrecht, c. 1460","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFuchs.margin_(MMW10F50_f6r)_detail.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Reineke.1498.jpg/500px-Reineke.1498.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Reineke.1498.jpg/500px-Reineke.1498.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 1498 illustration from Hans van Ghetelen, in Reinke de Vos","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AReineke.1498.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Reynard-the-fox.jpg/500px-Reynard-the-fox.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Reynard-the-fox.jpg/500px-Reynard-the-fox.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The trickster figure Reynard the Fox as depicted in an 1869 children's book by Michel Rodange","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AReynard-the-fox.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Renart_illumination.jpg/500px-Renart_illumination.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Renart_illumination.jpg/500px-Renart_illumination.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chanticleer and the Fox in a mediaeval manuscript miniature","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARenart_illumination.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Reynard_hunting_%28heaton_mid_19th_cent%29.jpg/500px-Reynard_hunting_%28heaton_mid_19th_cent%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Reynard_hunting_%28heaton_mid_19th_cent%29.jpg/500px-Reynard_hunting_%28heaton_mid_19th_cent%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Victorian stained-glass window by Clement James Heaton","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AReynard_hunting_(heaton_mid_19th_cent).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/RMF%2C_Kurhaus_Wiesbaden%2C_Chanticleer.jpg/500px-RMF%2C_Kurhaus_Wiesbaden%2C_Chanticleer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/R